        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: "A uterine isthmocele is a pouch-like defect in the uterine wall at the site of a prior cesarean section scar. When the myometrium does not heal completely after a C-section, a niche forms. Menstrual blood pools in this space, drains slowly, and creates local inflammation that can affect bleeding patterns, pelvic pain, and fertility. The condition goes by several names in the medical literature: cesarean scar defect, uterine niche, uterine diverticulum. All describe the same anatomical finding. Studies find some degree of defect in 24 to 70% of women after C-section on standard ultrasound, and in up to 84% when saline infusion sonohysterography is used. (Kremer et al., 2019) Consult an RRM clinician or healthcare provider for guidance specific to your situation.",
      },
