        text: "The hallmark isthmocele symptom is postmenstrual brown spotting - old-looking blood that persists for more than two days after the main period ends. The isthmocele acts as a reservoir during menstruation; blood accumulates, and fibrotic tissue prevents the uterus from clearing it promptly. Other common symptoms include pelvic pain or intense cramping, intermenstrual spotting, pain during intercourse, and secondary infertility. Many women with smaller defects are asymptomatic. Women who chart their cycles with Creighton or FEMM often document the spotting pattern before a formal diagnosis is made. Abnormal uterine bleeding occurs in 28.9 to 82% of women with isthmocele in published studies. (Baldini et al., 2024) Consult an RRM clinician or healthcare provider for guidance specific to your situation.",
