          <aside class="tldr">
            <h2 id="key-takeaways">Key Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Isthmocele is a pouch-like defect at the cesarean scar.</strong> It forms when the uterine wall does not heal flush after a C-section, leaving a niche that traps menstrual blood. (<a href="/library/isthmocele-an-overview-of-diagnosis-and-treatment-recxvsgc7hchpnw2m/">Kremer et al., 2019</a>)</li>
  <li><strong>Postmenstrual brown spotting is the hallmark symptom.</strong> Blood pools in the niche and drains slowly after menstruation ends, creating a pattern visible on a Creighton or FEMM cycle chart. (<a href="/library/uterine-isthmocele-a-frequently-overlooked-complication-of-cesarean-sections-recoinwrfjhmyev9w/">Rupa et al., 2021</a>)</li>
  <li><strong>Prevalence is higher than most clinicians expect.</strong> Studies find defects in 24 to 70% of women after a C-section on transvaginal ultrasound, and in 56 to 84% when saline infusion sonohysterography (SIS) is used. (<a href="/library/emerging-manifestations-of-cesarean-scar-defect-in-reproductive-aged-women-recrvbf5y6nqxayyt/">Tulandi and Cohen, 2016</a>)</li>
  <li><strong>An isthmocele impairs fertility through inflammation, fluid accumulation, and physical obstruction.</strong> The risk of infertility in women with isthmocele ranges from 4 to 19% in the literature. (<a href="/library/isthmocele-and-infertility-recwpl3h1tdyzcxia/">Baldini et al., 2024</a>)</li>
  <li><strong>The residual myometrial thickness drives the surgical decision.</strong> A commonly used expert-based threshold is that tissue greater than 2.5 to 3 mm typically supports hysteroscopic repair; thinner tissue generally leads surgeons toward laparoscopic excision with multilayer reconstruction or vaginal repair to restore the uterine wall. The appropriate approach is individualized with the surgical team. (<a href="/library/current-surgical-treatment-of-uterine-isthmocele-an-update-of-existing-literatur-reckgfhmdj201uq1e/">Stavridis et al., 2024</a>)</li>
  <li><strong>Surgical repair restores natural fertility and resolves abnormal bleeding.</strong> Small studies and pooled case series suggest pregnancy in roughly half to two-thirds of women after surgical repair; high-quality comparative trials remain limited. (<a href="/library/isthmocele-and-infertility-recwpl3h1tdyzcxia/">Baldini et al., 2024</a>)</li>
</ul>

